#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""architecture-diagram-check: the committed page must be what the model says.

WHY THIS EXISTS. The architecture page is generated from architecture/workspace.dsl.
A published diagram that is quietly wrong is worse than no diagram, because it is read
as current: a page that still names a retired component, or whose mermaid fences never
close, looks exactly like a page that is current. GitHub renders a page whose fences do
not close as ONE unterminated code block, so the diagrams do not render at all.

WHAT IT CHECKS. Ten things, all decided from BYTES -- no docker, no network, and no
rendering, so it runs in seconds on every push and on a laptop:

  1. FENCES. Every mermaid block opens and closes, and no fence is glued to other text.
     (structurizr writes each .mmd with NO trailing newline, so a renderer that does
     `cat "$f"; echo '```'` produces "  end```" -- and the page stops rendering.)
  2. VIEWS. The set of views on the page is exactly the set the model declares. A view
     added to the model and not regenerated is drift; a view deleted from the model and
     still on the page is drift.
  3. ELEMENTS, PER VIEW, WITH MULTIPLICITY. The elements drawn in each view are exactly
     the elements the model puts in that view -- and each of them ONCE. A page that
     simply DROPS a declared element -- the guardian, the gate -- is drift, and so is a
     page that draws the same box twice.
  4. NODE TEXT, PER ELEMENT. Every node is compared against THE DECLARATION OF THAT ONE
     ELEMENT -- not against the model file as a bag of words. The renderer presents an
     element as
         <div style='font-weight: bold'>NAME</div>
         <div style='font-size: 70%; ...'>[Kind] or [Kind: description]</div>
         <div style='font-size: 80%; ...'>body</div>
     and the model's declaration for an element is
         alias = kind "Name" "first string" ["second string"]
     so, measured against the committed page and the committed model:
       - the BODY div carries the element's FIRST string after its name;
       - the MARKER carries the element's SECOND string only for a CONTAINER
         (a container's description is its identity in a container view); for a person
         and for a software system the marker carries no description;
       - the marker's KIND label is the element's own kind.
     Comparing against the whole model file instead of the element's own declaration is
     the difference between "is this the model's claim about THIS box" and "is this
     sentence somewhere in the model" -- and only the first one catches a page that
     swaps two elements' bodies, or relabels a person as a container.
  5. THE SYSTEM BOUNDARY. In a container view the renderer nests the software system's
     own containers in a subgraph named after that system. A page that moves a software
     system INSIDE that boundary -- or a container OUTSIDE it -- draws a C4 boundary
     the model does not declare, and no per-box text check can see it.
  6. THE VIEW'S OWN TITLE. Each view's mermaid block opens with a subgraph labelled
     "<Kind> View: <the system the view is of>". A page that relabels that subgraph
     claims the diagram is a view of something else.
  7. EDGE LABELS AND TOPOLOGY. The renderer draws ONE edge per pair of elements, and
     when several relationships project onto the same pair it draws that pair once,
     labelled with the FIRST of them the model declares. So the page must draw exactly
     one edge per projected pair, with that label: a label that is not the model's is
     drift, a declared relationship that is missing from the page is drift, a
     relationship the model does not declare is drift, an edge re-pointed at a
     different element is drift, and a second edge for one pair is drift.
  8. NAMES. Every element name the model declares is drawn, and every name on the page
     is declared -- so a renamed element cannot leave half the page talking about the
     old name.
  9. THE HEADER. The page's header is exactly what architecture/render writes, read
     FROM the render, so there is one source of truth and the render cannot change it
     silently.
  10. NO SILENCE. A line inside a mermaid block that this file has no rule for is
     reported as UNCHECKED and the run FAILS. So is a view kind with no rule here, a
     node this file cannot read, and a page whose header this file cannot derive from
     architecture/render. A check that says "ok" about something it did not look at is
     not a check: a parser that DROPS what it does not understand turns "the page says
     something I cannot read" into "the page says nothing", and that is how a box for
     an element that does not exist sails through.

WHAT THIS CHECK DOES NOT COMPARE, STATED PLAINLY SO IT IS NOT A HIDDEN CLAIM:
  - The SECOND string of a person or of a software system. The renderer DROPS it (the
    marker carries no description for those kinds), so the page cannot show it and no
    page could satisfy a check that demanded it. A model that puts a description there
    is relying on a string nobody can read.
  - Node COLOURS. The renderer derives a style line per node from the model's styles
    block; this file does not re-derive structurizr's colour maths. The node's KIND is
    already compared, in the marker text.
  - Edge SHAPE and direction, beyond the endpoints and the label.
  - Whether the .mmd files the renderer produces still match the model. That is what
    `architecture/render --check` is for, and it needs the structurizr image.
  - The page's PROSE after the header (the renderer's own trailing line). Only the
    header is compared; the trailer is not.

`architecture/render --check` is the byte-exact check (it re-renders and diffs) and
needs the structurizr image. This one needs nothing, so it can run everywhere.

Usage: python3 bin/architecture-diagram-check
Exit 0 = the page is what the model says. Exit 1 = it is not. Exit 2 = it could not be
decided (something could not be read, or a view kind has no rule here).
"""

from __future__ import annotations

import os
import re
import subprocess
import sys

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- the model ---

# An element declaration: `alias = kind "Name" ["first string"] ["second string"]`.
# The match is a PREFIX match on purpose: a block declaration ends with `{`.
ELEMENT_RE = re.compile(
    r'^\s*([a-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*)\s*=\s*(person|softwareSystem|container)\s+"([^"]*)"'
    r'(?:\s+"([^"]*)")?(?:\s+"([^"]*)")?'
)
VIEW_RE = re.compile(
    r'^\s*(systemContext|container|component|deployment|dynamic)\s+'
    r'([A-Za-z0-9_.-]+)\s+"([^"]+)"(?:\s+"([^"]+)")?\s*\{'
)
INCLUDE_RE = re.compile(r'^\s*include\s+(\S+)\s*$')
RELATIONSHIP_RE = re.compile(
    r'^\s*([A-Za-z0-9_]+(?:\.[A-Za-z0-9_]+)*)\s*->\s*'
    r'([A-Za-z0-9_]+(?:\.[A-Za-z0-9_]+)*)\s+"([^"]*)"\s*$'
)
# A deployment environment and the instances it declares. A deployment view draws the
# INSTANCES an environment declares, so the elements a deployment view draws are the
# ones the model instantiates there.
DEPLOYMENT_ENV_RE = re.compile(r'^\s*deploymentEnvironment\s+"([^"]*)"\s*\{')
# A line that LOOKS like a declaration (`alias = ...`) but that this parser cannot read.
# The model is full of DSL this file has no rule for -- views, styles, tags, properties
# -- so the shape alone cannot be an error. But a line that assigns something is a
# declaration, and a declaration this file cannot read is an element it will silently
# leave out of every comparison.
DECLARATION_RE = re.compile(r'^\s*[^\s=]+\s*=\s*\S+')
# The alias charset above is deliberately wider than the one ELEMENT_RE accepts:
# a declaration this file cannot read must still be REPORTED, not dropped.  Structurizr
# accepts identifiers that start with a digit, so `1ghost = container "X"` is a real
# model, and dropping it silently left the element out of every comparison.
INSTANCE_RE = re.compile(r'^\s*(containerInstance|instanceOf)\s+([A-Za-z0-9_.]+)\s*$')
# A deployment node is a NESTED box in a deployment view: the model declares it, and the
# renderer draws it as a subgraph named after it. A check that says "every subgraph must
# be the view title or the system boundary" would reject a correct deployment view.
DEPLOYMENT_NODE_RE = re.compile(r'^\s*deploymentNode\s+"([^"]*)"')

# The kind label the renderer puts in a node's marker. This is the renderer's own
# vocabulary, keyed by the element's OWN kind -- not a set of words that happen to
# appear somewhere in the model file.
KIND_LABEL = {
    "person": "Person",
    "softwareSystem": "Software System",
    "container": "Container",
}

# Which kind carries its second string (its description) inside the marker.
CARRIES_MARKER_DESCRIPTION = {"container": True, "person": False, "softwareSystem": False}

# The label the renderer puts on the subgraph that opens a view, per view kind. The
# label names the SYSTEM the view is of, not the view's key -- which is why two views
# of the same system carry the same label.
VIEW_LABEL_PREFIX = {
    "systemContext": "System Context View",
    "container": "Container View",
    "deployment": "Deployment View",
}


def _collapse(text: str) -> str:
    return re.sub(r"\s+", " ", text).strip()


def _plain(text: str) -> str:
    """The renderer's HTML to plain text.

    ONLY the renderer's own tags go. Angle brackets that are TEXT -- a description that
    names a path template like `secrets/<env>/<name>.yaml` -- are not tags, and eating
    them turns a claim the model makes into one the model does not, which is the exact
    failure this check exists to catch.
    """
    text = text.replace("<br />", " ")
    text = re.sub(r"</?div[^>]*>", " ", text)
    return text


class Element:
    """One element the model declares."""

    def __init__(self, alias: str, kind: str, name: str, first: str, second: str) -> None:
        self.alias = alias
        self.kind = kind
        self.name = name
        self.first = first          # the first string after the name: the body the renderer shows
        self.second = second        # the second string after the name: a container's description

    @property
    def label(self) -> str:
        return KIND_LABEL.get(self.kind, self.kind)

    @property
    def carries_marker_description(self) -> bool:
        return CARRIES_MARKER_DESCRIPTION.get(self.kind, False)


class Model:
    """The model: its elements, their parents, its relationships, and each view."""

    def __init__(self, text: str) -> None:
        self.text = text
        self.elements: dict[str, Element] = {}     # alias -> Element
        self.by_name: dict[str, Element] = {}      # name  -> Element
        self.alias_of: dict[str, str] = {}         # name  -> alias
        self.parent: dict[str, str] = {}           # alias -> enclosing alias
        self.children: dict[str, list[str]] = {}    # software system alias -> child aliases
        self.views: list[dict] = []
        # DECLARATION ORDER matters: when several relationships project onto one pair,
        # the renderer draws that pair ONCE, labelled with the FIRST of them.
        self.relationships: list[tuple[str, str, str]] = []
        self.unparsed_relationships: list[str] = []
        self.duplicate_names: list[str] = []
        self.unreadable_declarations: list[str] = []
        self.deployments: dict[str, list[str]] = {}   # environment title -> element aliases
        self.deployment_nodes: dict[str, list[str]] = {}  # environment title -> node names
        self.deployment_opaque: list[str] = []        # environments this file cannot read
        self._parse()

    def _parse(self) -> None:
        lines = self.text.split("\n")
        stack: list[str] = []          # what each open block is (an alias, or a marker)
        in_views = False
        for raw in lines:
            line = raw.rstrip()
            stripped = line.strip()

            if not stripped or stripped.startswith("//") or stripped.startswith("*"):
                continue
            if stripped == "}":
                if stack:
                    stack.pop()
                continue
            if stripped.startswith("views") and stripped.endswith("{"):
                in_views = True
                stack.append("#block")
                continue

            push: str | None = None
            if not in_views:
                dem = DEPLOYMENT_ENV_RE.match(line)
                if dem:
                    self.deployments.setdefault(dem.group(1), [])
                    push = "#deployment:" + dem.group(1)
                else:
                    dnode = DEPLOYMENT_NODE_RE.match(line)
                    if dnode:
                        env = next(
                            (s for s in reversed(stack) if s.startswith("#deployment:")), None
                        )
                        if env is not None:
                            self.deployment_nodes.setdefault(env[len("#deployment:"):], []).append(
                                dnode.group(1)
                            )
                        # NOT a `continue`: the line ends with `{`, and the generic push
                        # below must open the node's block -- skipping it would leave the
                        # closing `}` to pop the ENVIRONMENT's marker instead, and every
                        # instance after it would land in the wrong environment.
                    inst = INSTANCE_RE.match(line)
                    if inst:
                        # The instance may sit inside nested deploymentNode blocks, so
                        # the environment it belongs to is the nearest one on the stack.
                        env = next(
                            (s for s in reversed(stack) if s.startswith("#deployment:")), None
                        )
                        if env is not None:
                            self.deployments[env[len("#deployment:"):]].append(inst.group(2))
                        continue
                    m = ELEMENT_RE.match(line)
                    if m:
                        alias, kind, name, first, second = m.groups()
                        element = Element(alias, kind, name, first or "", second or "")
                        self.elements[alias] = element
                        if name in self.by_name:
                            self.duplicate_names.append(name)
                        self.by_name.setdefault(name, element)
                        self.alias_of.setdefault(name, alias)
                        if stack and not stack[-1].startswith("#"):
                            self.parent[alias] = stack[-1]
                        for owner in stack:
                            if not owner.startswith("#"):
                                self.children.setdefault(owner, []).append(alias)
                        if stripped.endswith("{"):
                            push = alias
                    else:
                        rm = RELATIONSHIP_RE.match(line)
                        if rm:
                            source, target, label = rm.groups()
                            from_name = self.resolve(source)
                            to_name = self.resolve(target)
                            if from_name is None or to_name is None:
                                self.unparsed_relationships.append(line.strip())
                            else:
                                self.relationships.append((from_name, to_name, _collapse(label)))
                        elif DECLARATION_RE.match(line):
                            self.unreadable_declarations.append(line.strip())
            else:
                vm = VIEW_RE.match(line)
                if vm:
                    kind, ident, title, key = vm.groups()
                    name = key if kind == "deployment" and key else title
                    self.views.append(
                        {
                            "kind": kind,
                            "ident": ident,
                            "name": name,
                            "title": title,
                            "includes": [],
                        }
                    )
                    push = "#block"
                else:
                    im = INCLUDE_RE.match(line)
                    if im and self.views:
                        self.views[-1]["includes"].append(im.group(1))
                        continue
                    if DECLARATION_RE.match(line):
                        # A declaration inside the views block is not a view, a view
                        # include, or a relationship: this file cannot read it.
                        self.unreadable_declarations.append(line.strip())

            if push is None and stripped.endswith("{"):
                push = "#block"
            if push is not None:
                stack.append(push)

    def resolve(self, ident: str) -> str | None:
        """`genesis.guardian` -> the name of the element declared as `guardian`."""
        alias = ident.split(".")[-1]
        element = self.elements.get(alias)
        return element.name if element else None

    def nearest_visible(self, name: str, visible: set[str]) -> str | None:
        """The element the renderer draws for `name` in a view that draws `visible`.

        A view that does not draw an element draws the nearest ancestor that it DOES
        draw: a systemContext view draws the software system itself and not its
        containers, so a relationship to one of those containers is drawn against the
        system. That is the renderer's projection, and the page is compared against the
        projection -- otherwise every container relationship in the context view would
        read as drift, and a check that cries wolf is a check nobody runs.
        """
        if name in visible:
            return name
        alias = self.alias_of.get(name)
        while alias:
            parent_alias = self.parent.get(alias)
            if not parent_alias:
                return None
            parent = self.elements.get(parent_alias)
            if parent is None:
                return None
            if parent.name in visible:
                return parent.name
            alias = parent_alias
        return None

    def descendants_of(self, alias: str) -> set[str]:
        """The names of every element inside `alias` in the model's own hierarchy."""
        out: set[str] = set()
        for child_alias in self.children.get(alias, []):
            element = self.elements.get(child_alias)
            if element is None:
                continue
            out.add(element.name)
            out |= self.descendants_of(child_alias)
        return out

    def projected_relationships(self, visible: set[str]) -> set[tuple[str, str, str]]:
        """The relationships the renderer can draw in a view that draws `visible`."""
        projected: set[tuple[str, str, str]] = set()
        for source, target, label in self.relationships:
            near_source = self.nearest_visible(source, visible)
            near_target = self.nearest_visible(target, visible)
            if near_source is None or near_target is None:
                continue
            projected.add((near_source, near_target, label))
        return projected

    def expected_for(self, view: dict) -> tuple[set[str] | None, str]:
        """The elements the model puts in this view, or (None, why) if unknowable."""
        includes = view["includes"]
        if includes and includes != ["*"]:
            names = set()
            for ident in includes:
                resolved = self.resolve(ident)
                if resolved is None:
                    return None, "an include names %r, which the model does not declare" % ident
                names.add(resolved)
            return names, ""
        if includes != ["*"]:
            return None, "the view declares no include"
        ident = view["ident"]
        owner = ident.split(".")[-1]
        if owner not in self.elements:
            return None, "the view names %r, which the model does not declare" % ident
        # The rule is the VIEW's kind, not the element's: `container genesis` is a view of
        # the software system's children, and `systemContext genesis` is a view of the
        # system plus everything outside it.
        if view["kind"] == "container":
            # `container S` with include * draws S's children and the elements they touch,
            # but not S itself.
            return {e.name for e in self.elements.values()} - {self.elements[owner].name}, ""
        if view["kind"] == "systemContext":
            # `systemContext S` with include * draws S and everything OUTSIDE it.
            inside = {self.elements[a].name for a in self.children.get(owner, [])}
            if not inside:
                return None, "the model does not say which elements are inside %r" % ident
            outside = {e.name for e in self.elements.values()} - inside
            return outside | {self.elements[owner].name}, ""
        if view["kind"] == "deployment":
            # A deployment view draws the INSTANCES its environment declares, so the
            # elements it draws are the ones the model instantiates in that environment.
            title = view.get("title") or ""
            aliases = self.deployments.get(title)
            if aliases is None:
                return None, "the model declares no deployment environment titled %r" % title
            names = set()
            for alias in aliases:
                element = self.elements.get(alias.split(".")[-1])
                if element is None:
                    return None, (
                        "the deployment environment %r instantiates %r, which the model "
                        "does not declare" % (title, alias)
                    )
                names.add(element.name)
            return names, ""
        return None, "a %s view with include * is not a rule this check knows" % view["kind"]

    def view_label(self, view: dict) -> str | None:
        """The label the renderer puts on the subgraph that opens this view."""
        prefix = VIEW_LABEL_PREFIX.get(view["kind"])
        owner = view["ident"].split(".")[-1]
        element = self.elements.get(owner)
        if prefix is None or element is None:
            return None
        if view["kind"] == "deployment":
            title = view.get("title") or ""
            return "%s: %s - %s" % (prefix, element.name, title)
        return "%s: %s" % (prefix, element.name)


# ----------------------------------------------------------------- the page ---

FENCE_OPEN = re.compile(r"^## (.+)$")
FENCE_CLOSE = re.compile(r"^[ \t]*```[ \t]*$")

# The renderer's whole vocabulary inside a mermaid block, measured from the committed
# pages. The id shape is the renderer's: nodes are numbered, but the view's own
# subgraph is called `diagram`, so ids are not numeric.
ID = r"[A-Za-z0-9_][A-Za-z0-9_]*"
NODE_RE = re.compile(r'^\s*(' + ID + r')\["([^"]*)"\]\s*$')
EDGE_RE = re.compile(
    r'^\s*(' + ID + r')-\.?\s*"([^"]*)"\s*(?:\.-+>|-->|-.+->)\s*(' + ID + r')\s*$'
)
GRAPH_RE = re.compile(r"^\s*(?:graph|flowchart)\s+[A-Za-z]{1,3}\s*$")
LINKSTYLE_RE = re.compile(r"^\s*linkStyle\s+\S+.*$")
SUBGRAPH_RE = re.compile(r'^\s*subgraph\s+(' + ID + r')\s*\["(.*)"\]\s*$')
STYLE_RE = re.compile(r"^\s*style\s+(" + ID + r")\s+.*$")
END_RE = re.compile(r"^\s*end\s*$")

DIV_RE = re.compile(r"<div\b[^>]*>(.*?)</div>", re.S)
MARKER_RE = re.compile(r"^\[(.*)\]$", re.S)


class Node:
    """One element drawn on the page."""

    def __init__(self, ident: str, raw: str) -> None:
        self.ident = ident
        self.name = ""
        self.marker = ""       # the marker text without its brackets, e.g. "Container: ..."
        self.marker_label = ""  # the kind label, e.g. "Container"
        self.marker_description = ""  # the description inside the marker, if any
        self.body = ""
        self.readable = True
        self.reason = ""

        divs = DIV_RE.findall(raw)
        if len(divs) != 3:
            self.readable = False
            self.reason = "the node has %d divs, not the 3 the renderer writes" % len(divs)
            return
        name_div, marker_div, body_div = divs
        self.name = _collapse(_plain(name_div))
        marker_text = _collapse(_plain(marker_div))
        mm = MARKER_RE.match(marker_text)
        if not mm:
            self.readable = False
            self.reason = "the node's marker %r is not a [Kind] marker" % marker_text
            return
        self.marker = mm.group(1)
        self.marker_label, _, description = self.marker.partition(":")
        self.marker_label = self.marker_label.strip()
        self.marker_description = _collapse(description)
        self.body = _collapse(_plain(body_div))


class Page:
    """The page: per view, the nodes drawn, the edges between them, and the subgraphs.

    The parser knows the renderer's WHOLE vocabulary. A line it does not recognise is
    not dropped: it is recorded as unreadable, and an unreadable line makes the run
    UNCHECKED rather than ok. A parser that drops what it does not understand turns
    "the page says something I cannot read" into "the page says nothing".
    """

    def __init__(self, text: str) -> None:
        self.view_names: list[str] = []
        self.nodes: dict[str, dict[str, Node]] = {}   # view -> id -> Node
        self.edges: dict[str, list[tuple[str, str, str]]] = {}  # view -> (from, to, label)
        self.subgraphs: dict[str, list[dict]] = {}    # view -> {id, label, members}
        self.unreadable: list[str] = []
        self.duplicates: list[str] = []
        current: str | None = None
        in_block = False
        orphan = False
        open_subgraphs: list[dict] = []
        for line in text.split("\n"):
            fm = FENCE_OPEN.match(line)
            if fm and in_block:
                # A heading INSIDE a mermaid block is not a heading: the fence is
                # still open, so the line is part of the diagram.  It has no rule
                # here, so it is reported -- and the block is NOT ended, so the
                # lines after it are still read.
                self.unreadable.append(
                    "%s: a heading inside a mermaid block: %s" % (current, fm.group(1).strip())
                )
                continue
            if fm:
                current = fm.group(1).strip()
                if current not in self.nodes:
                    self.view_names.append(current)
                    self.nodes[current] = {}
                    self.edges[current] = []
                    self.subgraphs[current] = []
                open_subgraphs = []
                in_block = False
                continue
            if not in_block:
                if line.strip() == "```mermaid":
                    in_block = True
                    if current is None:
                        # A block with no view heading: there is nothing to compare it
                        # against, so it is reported and its lines are not read.
                        self.unreadable.append(
                            "a mermaid block appears before any view heading"
                        )
                        orphan = True
                continue
            if FENCE_CLOSE.match(line):
                in_block = False
                orphan = False
                if open_subgraphs:
                    self.unreadable.append(
                        "a subgraph is still open when the mermaid block closes"
                    )
                    open_subgraphs = []
                continue
            if orphan:
                continue

            stripped = line.strip()
            if not stripped:
                continue
            nm = NODE_RE.match(line)
            if nm:
                node = Node(nm.group(1), nm.group(2))
                if not node.readable:
                    self.unreadable.append(
                        "%s: node %s -- %s" % (current, nm.group(1), node.reason)
                    )
                if nm.group(1) in self.nodes[current]:
                    # Two node lines with the SAME id: mermaid draws the box twice, so
                    # the page shows the element twice. Keeping only the last one would
                    # make that invisible.
                    self.duplicates.append(
                        "%s: the page draws node %s twice" % (current, nm.group(1))
                    )
                self.nodes[current][nm.group(1)] = node
                for subgraph in open_subgraphs:
                    subgraph["members"].add(nm.group(1))
                continue
            em = EDGE_RE.match(line)
            if em:
                label = _collapse(_plain(em.group(2)))
                self.edges[current].append((em.group(1), em.group(3), label))
                continue
            sm = SUBGRAPH_RE.match(line)
            if sm:
                subgraph = {"id": sm.group(1), "label": sm.group(2), "members": set()}
                self.subgraphs[current].append(subgraph)
                open_subgraphs.append(subgraph)
                continue
            if END_RE.match(stripped):
                if not open_subgraphs:
                    self.unreadable.append(
                        "%s: an `end` closes a subgraph that was never opened" % current
                    )
                else:
                    open_subgraphs.pop()
                continue
            if GRAPH_RE.match(stripped) or LINKSTYLE_RE.match(stripped) or STYLE_RE.match(stripped):
                continue
            self.unreadable.append(
                "%s: a line inside the mermaid block that this check has no rule for: %s"
                % (current, stripped[:80])
            )

    def names_in(self, view: str) -> list[str]:
        """The names drawn in a view, WITH multiplicity: a box drawn twice is drift."""
        return [node.name for node in self.nodes.get(view, {}).values()]

    def subgraph_labelled(self, view: str, label: str) -> dict | None:
        for subgraph in self.subgraphs.get(view, []):
            if subgraph["label"] == label:
                return subgraph
        return None


def expected_header(render_path: str) -> tuple[list[str] | None, str]:
    """The header architecture/render writes, read FROM architecture/render.

    The header is a promise the render makes on every line of the page. Deriving the
    expected text from the render itself keeps ONE source of truth: change the render
    and this check compares against the new text, instead of against a second copy
    that can drift away from the first.
    """
    try:
        with open(render_path, encoding="utf-8") as handle:
            text = handle.read()
    except OSError:
        return None, "architecture/render is missing, so the page's header cannot be checked"
    block = re.search(r"render_page\(\)\s*\{(.*?)\n\}", text, re.S)
    if not block:
        return None, "architecture/render has no render_page() block"
    lines: list[str] = []
    started = False
    for raw in block.group(1).split("\n"):
        stripped = raw.strip()
        if not started:
            # The block opens with blank lines -- and, in some renders, a `{` that
            # groups the writes -- before the header writes themselves.
            if not stripped or stripped == "{":
                continue
            if not stripped.startswith("echo"):
                return None, "architecture/render does not start the page with a header"
            started = True
        if stripped.startswith("echo "):
            argument = stripped[5:].strip()
            if not (argument.startswith('"') and argument.endswith('"')):
                return None, "architecture/render echoes something that is not a quoted string"
            lines.append(argument[1:-1].replace("\\`", "`"))
        elif stripped == "echo":
            lines.append("")
        else:
            break
    if not lines:
        return None, "architecture/render writes no header"
    return lines, ""


def main() -> int:
    root = subprocess.run(
        ["git", "rev-parse", "--show-toplevel"], capture_output=True, text=True
    ).stdout.strip() or os.getcwd()
    model_path = os.path.join(root, "architecture", "workspace.dsl")
    page_path = os.path.join(root, "docs", "explanation", "architecture", "index.md")
    render_path = os.path.join(root, "architecture", "render")

    for path in (model_path, page_path):
        if not os.path.isfile(path):
            print("FAIL  architecture-diagram-check: %s is missing" % path, file=sys.stderr)
            return 2

    model = Model(open(model_path, encoding="utf-8").read())
    page = Page(open(page_path, encoding="utf-8").read())
    problems: list[str] = []
    undecided: list[str] = []

    # --- 1. fences ---------------------------------------------------------
    raw_page = open(page_path, encoding="utf-8").read()
    # Fence accounting, done IN ORDER and by BACKTICK WIDTH. A page may carry plain
    # code blocks as well as mermaid blocks, and a plain block's closing fence is not a
    # mermaid block's closer: counting every bare fence against the mermaid opens
    # rejects a page for having an ordinary ```bash block in it. And a block written
    # with FOUR backticks is a legal block, closed by four backticks -- so the width is
    # part of the rule, not an afterthought.
    FENCE_RUN_RE = re.compile(r"(`{3,})")
    mermaid_opens = 0
    mermaid_closes = 0
    block_opens = 0
    block_closes = 0
    open_block = None
    glued: list[str] = []
    for line in raw_page.split("\n"):
        run_match = FENCE_RUN_RE.search(line)
        if not run_match:
            continue
        run = run_match.group(1)
        before = line[: run_match.start()].strip()
        after = line[run_match.end():]
        if open_block is None:
            block_opens += 1
            info = after.rstrip()
            if len(info.split()) > 1:
                # The renderer never writes an info string with a space in it.
                glued.append(line)
            open_block = {"width": len(run), "mermaid": info.strip() == "mermaid"}
            if open_block["mermaid"]:
                mermaid_opens += 1
            continue
        if len(run) >= open_block["width"] and not before and not after.strip():
            block_closes += 1
            if open_block["mermaid"]:
                mermaid_closes += 1
            open_block = None
            continue
        if before:
            # A fence glued to text -- "  end```" -- never closes the block.
            glued.append(line)
    if open_block is not None:
        problems.append(
            "%s opens a code block it never closes -- the page will not render" % page_path
        )
    if mermaid_opens != mermaid_closes:
        problems.append(
            "%s opens %d mermaid blocks and closes %d -- the page will not render"
            % (page_path, mermaid_opens, mermaid_closes)
        )
    if block_opens != block_closes:
        problems.append(
            "%s opens %d code blocks and closes %d -- the page will not render"
            % (page_path, block_opens, block_closes)
        )
    if glued:
        problems.append("a mermaid fence is not alone on its line: " + " | ".join(glued))

    # --- 2. views ----------------------------------------------------------
    model_view_names = sorted({view["name"] for view in model.views})
    page_view_names = sorted(set(page.view_names))
    if model_view_names != page_view_names:
        only_model = [v for v in model_view_names if v not in page_view_names]
        only_page = [v for v in page_view_names if v not in model_view_names]
        problems.append(
            "the page's views and the model's views differ. only in the model: %s | only on the page: %s"
            % (" ".join(only_model), " ".join(only_page))
        )

    # --- 3. elements, per view, with multiplicity ---------------------------
    for view in model.views:
        expected, why = model.expected_for(view)
        if expected is None:
            undecided.append("%s -- %s" % (view["name"], why))
            continue
        drawn = page.names_in(view["name"])
        drawn_counts: dict[str, int] = {}
        for name in drawn:
            drawn_counts[name] = drawn_counts.get(name, 0) + 1
        expected_set = set(expected)
        if set(drawn_counts) != expected_set:
            problems.append(
                "the elements drawn in %s are not the model's: only in the model: %s | only on the page: %s"
                % (
                    view["name"],
                    " ".join(sorted(expected_set - set(drawn_counts))),
                    " ".join(sorted(set(drawn_counts) - expected_set)),
                )
            )
        for name, count in sorted(drawn_counts.items()):
            if count > 1:
                problems.append(
                    "the page draws %r %d times in %s; the model puts it there once"
                    % (name, count, view["name"])
                )

    # --- 4/5. node text, against THAT element's own declaration -----------
    for view_name, nodes in page.nodes.items():
        for node in nodes.values():
            if not node.readable:
                continue
            element = model.by_name.get(node.name)
            if element is None:
                problems.append(
                    "the page draws %r, which the model does not declare" % node.name
                )
                continue
            if node.marker_label != element.label:
                problems.append(
                    "%s is drawn as [%s] but the model declares it a %s"
                    % (node.name, node.marker_label, element.label)
                )
            if node.body != element.first:
                problems.append(
                    "%s shows %r but the model says %r"
                    % (node.name, node.body, element.first)
                )
            if element.carries_marker_description:
                # Only a model that DECLARES a description can be missing one. A
                # container declared with a single string has no description to draw,
                # and demanding one would make every page for such a model fail --
                # a check that can never be satisfied is not a check.
                if element.second:
                    if not node.marker_description:
                        problems.append(
                            "%s is drawn with no description but the model declares one"
                            % node.name
                        )
                    elif node.marker_description != element.second:
                        problems.append(
                            "%s shows the description %r but the model says %r"
                            % (node.name, node.marker_description, element.second)
                        )
                elif node.marker_description:
                    problems.append(
                        "%s is drawn with the description %r, but the model declares none"
                        % (node.name, node.marker_description)
                    )
            elif node.marker_description:
                problems.append(
                    "%s is drawn with the description %r, which the model does not put on a %s"
                    % (node.name, node.marker_description, element.label)
                )

    # --- 6. edges: one per projected pair, with the label the renderer picks ---
    for view in model.views:
        visible, why = model.expected_for(view)
        if visible is None:
            continue
        view_name = view["name"]
        # The relationships the model projects into this view, IN DECLARATION ORDER. A
        # relationship whose two endpoints project onto the SAME element is internal to
        # it and the renderer draws no edge for it; several relationships that project
        # onto the SAME PAIR are drawn as ONE edge, labelled with the FIRST of them.
        model_labels: dict[tuple[str, str], str] = {}
        expected_pairs: set[tuple[str, str]] = set()
        for source, target, label in model.relationships:
            near_source = model.nearest_visible(source, visible)
            near_target = model.nearest_visible(target, visible)
            if near_source is None or near_target is None or near_source == near_target:
                continue
            pair = (near_source, near_target)
            expected_pairs.add(pair)
            model_labels.setdefault(pair, label)
        drawn_pairs: dict[tuple[str, str], list[str]] = {}
        for source_id, target_id, label in page.edges.get(view_name, []):
            source = page.nodes.get(view_name, {}).get(source_id)
            target = page.nodes.get(view_name, {}).get(target_id)
            if source is None or target is None:
                problems.append(
                    "an edge in %s refers to a node that is not drawn there (%s -> %s)"
                    % (view_name, source_id, target_id)
                )
                continue
            pair = (source.name, target.name)
            drawn_pairs.setdefault(pair, []).append(label)
        for pair in sorted(expected_pairs - set(drawn_pairs)):
            problems.append(
                "the model declares a relationship the page does not draw in %s: %s -> %s"
                % (view_name, pair[0], pair[1])
            )
        for pair in sorted(set(drawn_pairs) - expected_pairs):
            problems.append(
                "the page draws a relationship the model does not declare in %s: %s -> %s"
                % (view_name, pair[0], pair[1])
            )
        for pair in sorted(set(drawn_pairs) & expected_pairs):
            labels = drawn_pairs[pair]
            if len(labels) > 1:
                problems.append(
                    "the page draws %d edges for %s -> %s in %s; the renderer draws one"
                    % (len(labels), pair[0], pair[1], view_name)
                )
            elif labels[0] != model_labels[pair]:
                problems.append(
                    "the page draws %s -> %s %r, but the model declares %r"
                    % (pair[0], pair[1], labels[0], model_labels[pair])
                )
    for name in sorted(set(model.duplicate_names)):
        undecided.append(
            "the model declares %r more than once; this check cannot tell those "
            "elements apart, so it cannot say which one the page draws" % name
        )
    for line in model.unreadable_declarations:
        undecided.append(
            "a declaration the model makes that this check cannot read: %s" % line
        )
    for line in model.unparsed_relationships:
        undecided.append("a relationship the model declares that this check cannot resolve: %s" % line)

    # --- 7. the system boundary, per container view -------------------------
    for view in model.views:
        if view["kind"] != "container":
            continue
        expected, why = model.expected_for(view)
        if expected is None:
            continue
        owner_alias = view["ident"].split(".")[-1]
        owner = model.elements.get(owner_alias)
        if owner is None:
            continue
        view_name = view["name"]
        boundaries = [
            s for s in page.subgraphs.get(view_name, []) if s["label"] == owner.name
        ]
        if not boundaries:
            problems.append(
                "the page does not draw the boundary of %s in %s" % (owner.name, view_name)
            )
            continue
        if len(boundaries) > 1:
            problems.append(
                "the page draws %d boundaries labelled %s in %s; the model declares one"
                % (len(boundaries), owner.name, view_name)
            )
        nodes = page.nodes.get(view_name, {})
        inside = {nodes[i].name for s in boundaries for i in s["members"] if i in nodes}
        descendants = model.descendants_of(owner_alias)
        for name in sorted(expected):
            if name == owner.name:
                continue
            drawn_inside = name in inside
            should_be_inside = name in descendants
            if drawn_inside != should_be_inside:
                problems.append(
                    "the page draws %s %s the boundary of %s in %s, but the model says %s"
                    % (
                        name,
                        "inside" if drawn_inside else "outside",
                        owner.name,
                        view_name,
                        "inside" if should_be_inside else "outside",
                    )
                )

    # --- 8. the view's own title ------------------------------------------
    for view in model.views:
        expected_label = model.view_label(view)
        if expected_label is None:
            undecided.append(
                "%s -- this check has no rule for the title of a %s view"
                % (view["name"], view["kind"])
            )
            continue
        labels = [s["label"] for s in page.subgraphs.get(view["name"], [])]
        if not labels or labels[0] != expected_label:
            problems.append(
                "the page draws %s as %r, but the renderer titles that view %r"
                % (view["name"], labels[0] if labels else "", expected_label)
            )
        else:
            prefix = expected_label.split(":")[0] + ":"
            for other in labels[1:]:
                if other.startswith(prefix):
                    problems.append(
                        "the page draws a second view title %r in %s; the renderer titles "
                        "that view %r" % (other, view["name"], expected_label)
                    )

    # --- 8b. every OTHER subgraph in the view -------------------------------
    # The renderer draws exactly two subgraphs in a container view -- the view's own
    # title and the system boundary -- and exactly one in a system context view.  Any
    # OTHER subgraph is a C4 boundary the model does not declare, whatever it is
    # labelled: a decoy named after another system, an extra grouping, a nested level
    # of nesting.  Comparing only the owner's own label let all of those through.
    for view in model.views:
        view_name = view["name"]
        owner_alias = view["ident"].split(".")[-1]
        owner = model.elements.get(owner_alias)
        declared = set()
        title = model.view_label(view)
        if title is not None:
            declared.add(title)
        if owner is not None and view["kind"] == "container":
            declared.add(owner.name)
        if view["kind"] == "deployment":
            # A deployment view nests the environment's own NODES as subgraphs.
            declared.update(model.deployment_nodes.get(view.get("title") or "", []))
        for s in page.subgraphs.get(view_name, []):
            if s["label"] not in declared:
                problems.append(
                    "the page draws a subgraph %r in %s; the model declares no such boundary"
                    % (s["label"], view_name)
                )

    # --- 9. the header the render writes ------------------------------------
    header, why = expected_header(render_path)
    if header is None:
        undecided.append(why)
    else:
        page_lines = raw_page.split("\n")
        actual = page_lines[: len(header)]
        if actual != header:
            problems.append(
                "the page's header is not what architecture/render writes: %r" % (actual[:2],)
            )

    # --- 10. element names, globally ----------------------------------------
    model_names = set(model.by_name)
    drawn_names = {name for view in page.nodes for name in page.names_in(view)}
    if model_names != drawn_names:
        problems.append(
            "the page's elements and the model's elements differ. only in the model: %s | only on the page: %s"
            % (
                " ".join(sorted(model_names - drawn_names)),
                " ".join(sorted(drawn_names - model_names)),
            )
        )

    # --- 11. nothing left undecided -----------------------------------------
    for note in page.duplicates:
        problems.append(note)
    for note in page.unreadable:
        undecided.append(note)

    if undecided:
        for note in undecided:
            print(
                "UNCHECKED  architecture-diagram-check: %s -- this check has no rule that "
                "decides it, so it cannot say the page is what the model says"
                % note,
                file=sys.stderr,
            )
    if problems:
        for problem in problems:
            print("FAIL  architecture-diagram-check: %s" % problem, file=sys.stderr)
        print(
            "  run 'architecture/render' to regenerate the page from architecture/workspace.dsl",
            file=sys.stderr,
        )
        return 1
    if undecided:
        return 2
    print("ok    architecture-diagram-check: %s is what %s says" % (page_path, model_path))
    return 0


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main())
